    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d630">

                <pb facs="0626" n="614" sameAs="frus:frus1937v01/pg614"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">852.01/295: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-11-13T11:00:00+09:00">November 13, 1937—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 13—1:18 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>540. The press states this morning that, in line with the desire of the Japanese
                    Government to implement the Anti-Communist Pact, <pb facs="0627" n="615"
                        xml:id="pg615"/> consideration is being given to Japan’s recognition of the
                    Franco regime.<anchor n="20" xml:id="d630-f1"/></p>
                <p>In connection with the negotiations which have been taking place for several
                    months with regard to the new commercial convention between Japan and Italy,
                    there are persistent indications that the possibilities of a trade arrangement
                    among Japan, Germany and Italy are being examined. It has been suggested that
                    such an arrangement would cover only raw and semi-manufactured materials, but
                    this is probably conjecture.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
